<DocsLayout title="Typed project memory in Haft v9" description="EntityOfConcern identity, bounded neighborhoods, project profiles, reliance-gated persistence, and the distinctions that keep memory honest.">
  <h1>Memory &amp; core concepts</h1>

  <p>
    Haft v9 stores project knowledge as typed relations around an exact
    <code>EntityOfConcern</code>. It does not treat a transcript, a markdown
    file, or a ranked search result as project truth by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Strict distinctions</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Object ≠ description ≠ carrier.</strong> A markdown file can carry a record; it is not the object the record describes.</li>
    <li><strong>Plan ≠ performed work.</strong> A WorkPlan can order intended actions; it does not execute them.</li>
    <li><strong>Recommendation ≠ decision.</strong> Binding requires a direct, unambiguous operator choice through the correct effect sink.</li>
    <li><strong>Claim ≠ evidence.</strong> A test description or expected result is not proof that the test ran or passed.</li>
    <li><strong>Retrieval ≠ applicability.</strong> Candidate rank never grants identity, authority, or pattern selection.</li>
    <li><strong>Target system ≠ enabling system.</strong> The product being changed and the engineering arrangement changing it remain separate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>EntityOfConcern</h2>

  <p>
    An <code>EntityOfConcern</code> is the exact project object around which
    Haft reads or writes a bounded memory neighborhood. Identity uses a stable
    reference and bounded context. Similar names, paths, or retrieval scores do
    not merge two entities.
  </p>

  <p>The task-level read sequence is:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Resolve a known name or alias in one pinned project-memory snapshot.</li>
    <li>Select the exact identity from the current use, not candidate rank.</li>
    <li>Read the smallest neighborhood needed through a named projection profile and budget.</li>
    <li>Use bounded recall only inside that exact entity and project snapshot.</li>
  </ol>

  <pre><code>haft memory resolve --input-file resolve.json
haft memory neighborhood --input-file neighborhood.json
haft memory recall --input-file recall.json</code></pre>

  <p>
    The MCP equivalent uses <code>haft_query(action="memory")</code> with one of
    the closed <code>resolve</code>, <code>neighborhood</code>, or
    <code>recall</code> request shapes.
  </p>

  <h2>When memory is written</h2>

  <p>Persistence has two valid bases:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>the operator explicitly asks to save, record, bind, approve, commission, or otherwise mutate project memory;</li>
    <li>current work has a concrete receiving use such as handoff, replay, audit, automation, delayed evidence, or costly reversal.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The second basis can be inferred from current work. When it requires stable
    identity and the identity coordinates are recoverable,
    <code>haft_entity</code> establishes the minimum non-binding
    <code>EntityOfConcern</code> without a separate permission prompt.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>known_absent</code>, an empty graph, or generic future usefulness is
    not a persistence basis. Entity establishment cannot bind a decision,
    approve a specification, commission work, or establish evidence truth.
  </p>

  <h2>Project profile and onboarding</h2>

  <p>
    <code>haft init</code> installs default project memory automatically. It may
    admit an initial <code>detector_default</code> profile only from a complete,
    non-truncated, supported singleton detector result and only when no
    canonical or human-reviewed profile already exists.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mixed, multiple-scope, insufficient, truncated, or previously reviewed
    bases require one direct operator choice. Preparation creates only a
    non-binding review carrier. Application uses the readable onboarding
    effect; the operator never selects an internal memory schema.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong><code>ready</code> has a narrow meaning.</strong> Onboarding ready
      means that a canonical profile and structured project memory are
      available. It does not prove spec applicability, spec health, lifecycle
      state, evidence truth, or release readiness.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Compatible memory successors</h2>

  <p>
    A mutation-capable init or reconciliation path automatically activates
    every exact bundled <code>ProjectTypeEnv</code> successor proven compatible
    with the transaction-current head, graph, profile, assertions, projection
    profiles, and runtime. This records
    <code>compatible_successor_policy</code>, not an operator request.
  </p>

  <p>
    An incompatible, incomplete, stale, or underdetermined candidate leaves the
    current head unchanged and returns the blocker. Rollback or selection
    outside that predicate remains a separate operator effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Identity changes in v9</h2>

  <p>
    Public identity change is alias-only:
    <code>admit_alias</code> adds a context-bound alias and
    <code>supersede_alias</code> replaces one while retaining provenance.
    Reviewed entity merge and split are deferred to a later v9.x contract.
  </p>

  <h2>Memory, code, and carriers</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Typed memory orients around one exact project entity.</li>
    <li>The fused code graph orients around files, symbols, calls, and governing records.</li>
    <li>SQLite is the runtime structured store.</li>
    <li><code>.haft/*.md</code> files are reviewable exchange and publication carriers imported through explicit <code>haft sync</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These surfaces can refer to one another, but none silently becomes the
    other. A carrier does not act, and a nearby code record does not establish
    an EntityOfConcern identity.
  </p>

